# Cloudflare Pages custom headers.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# Rules here are applied to Function responses as well as static assets, and
# they win over headers a Function sets for the same name — so anything that
# needs to vary per response (e.g. cache directives on an error) must NOT be
# pinned here.

# Baseline hardening for every route.
#
# NOTE ON FRAMING — deliberately absent here. Cloudflare Pages *appends* rules
# from a more specific path to the ones matched by `/*`; it does not replace
# them. A page matching both `/*` and `/embed/*` therefore receives TWO
# Content-Security-Policy headers, and browsers enforce the INTERSECTION of
# every CSP present. So a `frame-ancestors 'self'` here is not overridable by
# `frame-ancestors *` below — it silently wins, and third-party embeds break.
# (Verified against production: both headers were served, and the embed became
# unframable.) The same append behavior produces two conflicting
# X-Frame-Options values.
#
# Since the embed widget is a real feature and this is a read-only site with no
# auth and no destructive controls, framing stays unrestricted rather than
# breaking embeds for a clickjacking guard that buys little here. Everything
# else in the policy still applies.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), interest-cohort=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'

# Embed routes are explicitly framable from any origin so newsrooms can drop
# the widget into a story. This only needs to be permissive — with no
# frame-ancestors in the `/*` policy above, nothing here has to be overridden.
/embed/*
  X-Frame-Options: ALLOWALL

# Public API: CORS only. The Function sets its own Cache-Control so it can use
# `no-store` on a 503 — pinning a cache directive here would override that and
# let the edge serve a transient upstream failure for five minutes to every
# consumer. CORS stays here because it's identical on every response, so a
# stale function bundle can't drop it.
/api/v1/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS

# Vite build artifacts have content-hashed filenames, so a given URL is
# immutable — the hash changes whenever the content does. Cache them for a
# year so repeat visitors skip the re-download entirely.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
